<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.13.4: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01974</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.13.4: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fc696584-2dd3-4c51-b69d-0c1b355a48e3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>For 1 - 4, use the following information:</para>
    
    <para><quote>A parabola crosses the \(x\)-axis at (-1,0) and (2,0). We want to write a quadratic equation with those \(x\)-intercepts. We can do this without graphing.</quote></para><para>When \(y=0\), what must \(x\) be?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>We know that (-1,0) and (2,0) are points on the graph when \(y=0\). So there are two possibilities for \(x\): \(x=-1\) and \(x=2\).</para>
    
    <para>\(x=-1\) and \(x=2\) are called the real solutions of the quadratic because they are the solutions of the equation when we set \(y=0\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Rewrite the equations from number 1 so they both equal zero.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x+1=0\) and \(x-2=0\).</para>
    
    <para>Because each of the equations equals zero, multiplying them together will also equal zero.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What equation do you get when you multiply the previous equations together?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((x+1)(x-2)=0 \)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Multiply the binomials.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x^2-x-2=0\)</para>
    
    <para>This answer can also give us the more general quadratic with those \(x\)-intercepts. We replace 0 with \(y\) to show that \(y\) varies and isn’t always 0. So, \(y=x^2-x-2\) is a parabola that crosses the \(x\)-axis at -1 and 2.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
</section>

<note class="self-check">
  <para>The solution to a quadratic equation is \(x = 4\) and \(x = - 8\). What is the equation?</para>
</note>

<section><para>When you write a quadratic in factored form, that helps you find the \(x\)-intercepts. 
  \(y=x^2+2x-3\)
  \(y=(x+3)(x-1)\)
</para>
  <para>When we set \(y=0\), we find the places where the parabola crosses the \(x\)-axis.</para>
  
  <para>\(0=(x+3)(x-1)\)</para>
  
  <para>So, \(0=x+3\) and \(0=x-1\)
  \(x=-3\) and 1.</para>
  
  <para>-3 and 1 are called the real solutions to the quadratic equation.</para>
  
  <para>We can also start with the real solutions and use them to write quadratic equations.</para>
  
  <para>When we know that \(x=-3\) and 1, then that means \(0=x+3\) and \(0=x-1\).</para>
  
  <para>So, \(0=(x+3)(x-1)\).</para>
  
  <para>And, using the FOIL method to multiply this out, we come to 
  \(0=x^2+2x-3.\)</para>
  
  <para>When \(y\) is not equal to zero, we have the following quadratic equation</para>
  
  <para>\(y=x^2+2x-3\), which is exactly where we started!</para>

  
  <!-- BEGIN TRY IT-->

  <note class="try look-at-me"/>
<title>Try It: Writing Quadratic Equations from Real Solutions</title>
<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
  
    <para>Write a quadratic from the real solutions \(x=1\) and \(x=7\) without graphing.</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
<para>Compare your answer: 
\(x^2-8x+7\)</para>

<para>\(x=1\) and \(x=7\) become \(x-1=0\) and \(x-7=0\). \(y=(x-1)(x-7)= x^2-x-7x+7= x^2-8x+7\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!-- END TRY IT --></section>
